## sn11.3
sn11.3:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: I have heard that on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
sn11.3:1.2 | S: There the Buddha addressed the mendicants, | T: There he addressed the monks:
sn11.3:1.3 | S: “Mendicants!” | T: “Monks!”
sn11.3:1.4 | S: “Venerable sir,” they replied. | T: “Yes, lord!” the monks responded to him.
sn11.3:1.5 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said:
sn11.3:2.1 | S: “Once upon a time, mendicants, a battle was fought between the gods and the titans. | T: “Once, monks, the devas & asuras were arrayed for battle.
sn11.3:2.2 | S: Then Sakka, lord of gods, addressed the gods of the thirty-three: | T: Then Sakka, lord of the devas, addressed the Devas of the Thirty-three:
sn11.3:3.1 | S: ‘Good fellows, when the gods are fighting, if you get scared or terrified, just look up at my banner’s crest. | T: ‘If, dear sirs, when the devas are engaged in battle, there should arise fear, terror, or horripilation, then on that occasion you should look up at the top of my standard.
sn11.3:3.2 | S: Then your fear and terror will go away. | T: For when you look up at the top of my standard, any fear, terror, or horripilation you may have will be abandoned.
sn11.3:4.1 | S: If you can’t see my banner’s crest, then look up at the banner’s crest of the Progenitor, king of gods. | T: “‘If you don’t look up at the top of my standard, then you should look up at the top of the standard of Pajāpati the deva-king.
sn11.3:4.2 | S: Then your fear and terror will go away. | T: For when you look up at the top of the standard of Pajāpati the deva-king, any fear, terror, or horripilation you may have will be abandoned.
sn11.3:5.1 | S: If you can’t see his banner’s crest, then look up at the banner’s crest of Varuṇa, king of gods. | T: “‘If you don’t look up at the top of the standard of Pajāpati the deva-king, then you should look up at the top of the standard of Varuṇa the deva-king.
sn11.3:5.2 | S: Then your fear and terror will go away. | T: For when you look up at the top of the standard of Varuṇa the deva-king, any fear, terror, or horripilation you may have will be abandoned.
sn11.3:6.1 | S: If you can’t see his banner’s crest, then look up at the banner’s crest of Īsāna, king of gods. | T: “‘If you don’t look up at the top of the standard of Varuṇa the deva-king, then you should look up at the top of the standard of Īsāna the deva-king.
sn11.3:6.2 | S: Then your fear and terror will go away.’ | T: For when you look up at the top of the standard of Īsāna the deva-king, any fear, terror, or horripilation you may have will be abandoned.
sn11.3:7.1 | S: However, when they look up at those banner’s crests their fear and terror might go away or it might not. | T: “Monks, in those who look up at the top of the standard of Sakka, lord of the devas; in those who look up at the top of the standard of Pajāpati the deva-king; in those who look up at the top of the standard of Varuṇa, the deva-king; or in those who look up at the top of the standard of Īsāna, the deva-king, any fear, terror, or horripilation they may have might be abandoned, or it might not.
sn11.3:8.1 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
sn11.3:8.2 | S: Because Sakka is not free of greed, hate, and delusion. He is fearful, scared, nervous, quick to flee. | T: Because Sakka, lord of the devas, is not free of passion, free of aversion, or free of delusion. He can be frightened, terrorized, cowardly, quick to flee.
sn11.3:9.1 | S: But, mendicants, I say this: | T: “But, monks, I tell you this:
sn11.3:9.2 | S: If you’ve gone to a wilderness, or to the root of a tree, or to an empty hut and you get scared or terrified, just recollect me: | T: If, when you have gone to the wilderness, to the foot of a tree, or to an empty dwelling, there should arise fear, terror, or horripilation, then on that occasion you should recollect me thus:
sn11.3:9.3 | S: ‘That Blessed One is perfected, a fully awakened Buddha, accomplished in knowledge and conduct, holy, knower of the world, supreme guide for those fit for training, teacher of gods and humans, awakened, blessed.’ | T: ‘Indeed, the Blessed One is worthy & rightly self-awakened, consummate in clear-knowing & conduct, well-gone, an expert with regard to the cosmos, unexcelled trainer of people fit to be tamed, teacher of devas & human beings, awakened, blessed.’
sn11.3:9.4 | S: Then your fear and terror will go away. | T: For when you recollect me, monks, any fear, terror, or horripilation you may have will be abandoned.
sn11.3:10.1 | S: If you can’t recollect me, then recollect the teaching: | T: “If you don’t recollect me, then you should recollect the Dhamma thus:
sn11.3:10.2 | S: ‘The teaching is well explained by the Buddha—apparent in the present life, immediately effective, inviting inspection, relevant, so that sensible people can know it for themselves.’ | T: ‘The Dhamma is well taught by the Blessed One, to be seen here & now, timeless, inviting verification, leading out, to be experienced by the observant for themselves.’
sn11.3:10.3 | S: Then your fear and terror will go away. | T: For when you recollect the Dhamma, monks, any fear, terror, or horripilation you may have will be abandoned.
sn11.3:11.1 | S: If you can’t recollect the teaching, then recollect the Saṅgha: | T: “If you cannot recollect the Dhamma, then you should recollect the Saṅgha thus:
sn11.3:11.2 | S: ‘The Saṅgha of the Buddha’s disciples is practicing the way that’s good, direct, systematic, and proper. It consists of the four pairs, the eight individual persons. This is the Saṅgha of the Buddha’s disciples that is worthy of offerings dedicated to the gods, worthy of hospitality, worthy of a religious donation, worthy of greeting with cupped palms, and is the supreme field of merit for the world.’ | T: ‘The Saṅgha of the Blessed One’s disciples who have practiced well, practiced straightforwardly, practice methodically, practiced masterfully, i. e., the four pairs, the eight-types (of noble ones): That is the Saṅgha of the Blessed One’s disciples—deserving of gifts, deserving of hospitality, deserving of offerings, deserving of respect, the unexcelled field of merit for the world.’
sn11.3:11.3 | S: Then your fear and terror will go away. | T: For when you recollect the Saṅgha, monks, any fear, terror, or horripilation you may have will be abandoned.
sn11.3:12.1 | S: Why is that? | T: “Why is that?
sn11.3:12.2 | S: Because the Realized One is free of greed, hate, and delusion. He is fearless, brave, bold, and stands his ground.” | T: Because the Tathāgata, worthy & rightly self-awakened, is free of passion, free of aversion, free of delusion. He is fearless, cannot be terrorized, bold, not quick to flee.”
sn11.3:12.3 | S: That is what the Buddha said. | T: This is what the Blessed One said.
sn11.3:12.4 | S: Then the Holy One, the Teacher, went on to say: | T: Having said this, the One Well-Gone, the Teacher, further said this:
sn11.3:13.1 | S: “In the wilderness, at a tree’s root, | T: “In wilderness, monks, at the foot of a tree,
sn11.3:13.2 | S: or an empty hut, O mendicants, | T: or in an empty dwelling,
sn11.3:13.3 | S: recollect the Buddha, | T: recollect the Buddha:
sn11.3:13.4 | S: and no fear will come to you. | T: You will have no fear.
sn11.3:14.1 | S: If you can’t recollect the Buddha— | T: If you don’t recall the Buddha—
sn11.3:14.2 | S: the eldest in the world, the captain of men— | T: chief of the world, the bull of men—
sn11.3:14.3 | S: then recollect the teaching, | T: then recollect the Dhamma,
sn11.3:14.4 | S: emancipating, well taught. | T: leading out, well taught.
sn11.3:15.1 | S: If you can’t recollect the teaching— | T: If you don’t recall the Dhamma—
sn11.3:15.2 | S: emancipating, well taught— | T: leading out, well taught—
sn11.3:15.3 | S: then recollect the Saṅgha, | T: then recollect the Saṅgha,
sn11.3:15.4 | S: the supreme field of merit. | T: the field of merit unexcelled.
sn11.3:16.1 | S: Thus recollecting the Buddha, | T: For those who have thus recalled the Buddha,
sn11.3:16.2 | S: the teaching, and the Saṅgha, mendicants, | T: Dhamma, & Saṅgha, monks,
sn11.3:16.3 | S: fear and terror | T: there will be no terror, horripilation, or fear.”
sn11.3:16.4 | S: and goosebumps will be no more.” | T: (none)

## sn22.88
