## sn22.76
sn22.76:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: Near Sāvatthī. There the Blessed One addressed the monks,
sn22.76:1.2 | S: “Mendicants, form is impermanent. | T: “Monks, form is inconstant.
sn22.76:1.3 | S: What’s impermanent is suffering. | T: Whatever is inconstant is stressful.
sn22.76:1.4 | S: What’s suffering is not-self. | T: Whatever is stressful is not-self.
sn22.76:1.5 | S: And what’s not-self should be truly seen with right understanding like this: ‘This is not mine, I am not this, this is not my self.’ | T: That is to be seen with right discernment as it has come to be: ‘This is not mine. This is not my self. This is not what I am.’
sn22.76:1.6 | S: Feeling … | T: “Feeling is inconstant…
sn22.76:1.7 | S: Perception … | T: “Perception is inconstant…
sn22.76:1.8 | S: Choices … | T: “Fabrications are inconstant…
sn22.76:1.9 | S: Consciousness is impermanent. | T: “Consciousness is inconstant.
sn22.76:1.10 | S: What’s impermanent is suffering. | T: Whatever is inconstant is stressful.
sn22.76:1.11 | S: What’s suffering is not-self. | T: Whatever is stressful is not-self.
sn22.76:1.12 | S: And what’s not-self should be truly seen with right understanding like this: ‘This is not mine, I am not this, this is not my self.’ | T: That is to be seen with right discernment as it has come to be: ‘This is not mine. This is not my self. This is not what I am.’
sn22.76:2.1 | S: Seeing this, a learned noble disciple grows disillusioned with form, feeling, perception, choices, and consciousness. | T: “Seeing thus, the instructed disciple of the noble ones grows disenchanted with form, disenchanted with feeling, disenchanted with perception, disenchanted with fabrications, disenchanted with consciousness.
sn22.76:2.2 | S: Being disillusioned, desire fades away. When desire fades away they’re freed. When they’re freed, they know they’re freed. | T: Disenchanted, he becomes dispassionate. Through dispassion, he is released. With release, there is the knowledge, ‘Released.’
sn22.76:2.3 | S: They understand: ‘Rebirth is ended, the spiritual journey has been completed, what had to be done has been done, there is nothing further for this place.’ | T: He discerns that ‘Birth is ended, the holy life fulfilled, the task done. There is nothing further for this world.’
sn22.76:2.4 | S: As far as there are abodes of sentient beings, even up until the pinnacle of existence, the perfected ones are the foremost and the best.” | T: “Monks, to whatever extent there are abodes of beings, to whatever extent there is the height of becoming, arahants are supreme, arahants are the best in the world.”
sn22.76:3.1 | S: That is what the Buddha said. | T: That is what the Blessed One said.
sn22.76:3.2 | S: Then the Holy One, the Teacher, went on to say: | T: Having said that, the One Well-Gone, the Teacher, said further:
sn22.76:4.1 | S: “Oh! How happy are the perfected ones! | T: “How happy are the arahants!
sn22.76:4.2 | S: Craving is not found in them, | T: Craving isn’t found in them.
sn22.76:4.3 | S: the conceit ‘I am’ is cut off, | T: Cut off is the conceit, ‘I am’;
sn22.76:4.4 | S: and the net of delusion is shattered. | T: burst, the net of delusion.
sn22.76:5.1 | S: They’ve attained imperturbability, | T: Having reached imperturbability,
sn22.76:5.2 | S: their minds are unclouded, | T: their minds are clear & disturbance-free.
sn22.76:5.3 | S: nothing in the world clings to them, | T: Unsullied in the world,
sn22.76:5.4 | S: manifesting divinity, undefiled. | T: Brahmā–become, effluent-free,
sn22.76:6.1 | S: Completely understanding the five aggregates, | T: comprehending the five aggregates,
sn22.76:6.2 | S: their domain is the seven good qualities. | T: ranging in seven true dhammas,
sn22.76:6.3 | S: Those true persons are praiseworthy, | T: praiseworthy men of integrity:
sn22.76:6.4 | S: the Buddha’s bosom sons. | T: They are the Buddha’s children, his sons.
sn22.76:7.1 | S: Endowed with the seven gems, | T: Consummate in the seven treasures,
sn22.76:7.2 | S: and trained in the three trainings, | T: trained in the threefold training,
sn22.76:7.3 | S: the great heroes live on, | T: these great heroes go wandering
sn22.76:7.4 | S: with fear and dread given up. | T: having left fear & terror behind.
sn22.76:8.1 | S: Endowed with ten factors, | T: Consummate in ten factors,
sn22.76:8.2 | S: those giants are immersed in samādhi. | T: great nāgas, concentrated,
sn22.76:8.3 | S: They are the best in the world, | T: they are the best in the world:
sn22.76:8.4 | S: craving is not found in them. | T: Craving isn’t found in them.
sn22.76:9.1 | S: The adept’s knowledge has arisen: | T: The adept’s knowledge has arisen in them:
sn22.76:9.2 | S: ‘This bag of bones is my last.’ | T: ‘This is the last body.’
sn22.76:9.3 | S: They are independent of others | T: As for the essence of the holy life, they don’t depend on others in that.
sn22.76:9.4 | S: in the core of the spiritual path. | T: (none)
sn22.76:10.1 | S: They do not waver due to the discriminations, | T: They don’t waver from conceits.
sn22.76:10.2 | S: they’re freed from future lives. | T: They’re released from further-becoming.
sn22.76:10.3 | S: They’ve reached the level of the tamed, | T: Having reached the level of the tamed,
sn22.76:10.4 | S: in the world, they’re the winners. | T: they have victory in the world.
sn22.76:11.1 | S: Above, below, all round, | T: Above, around, & below,
sn22.76:11.2 | S: relishing is not found in them. | T: delight isn’t found in them.
sn22.76:11.3 | S: They roar their lion’s roar: | T: They roar their lion’s roar:
sn22.76:11.4 | S: ‘The awakened are supreme in the world!’” | T: ‘In the world, the awakened are unexcelled!’”
sn22.76:11.5 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

## an11.17
an11.17:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, a cowherd with eleven factors can’t maintain and expand a herd of cattle. | T: “Monks, a cowherd endowed with eleven factors is incapable of looking after a herd so that it prospers & grows.
an11.17:1.2 | S: What eleven? | T: Which eleven?
an11.17:1.3 | S: It’s when a cowherd doesn’t know form, is unskilled in characteristics, doesn’t pick out flies’ eggs, doesn’t dress wounds, doesn’t spread smoke, doesn’t know the ford, doesn’t know satisfaction, doesn’t know the trail, is not skilled in pastures, milks dry, and doesn’t show extra respect to the bulls who are fathers and captains of the herd. | T: There is the case where a cowherd is not well-versed in forms (appearances), unskilled in characteristics, doesn’t pick out flies’ eggs, doesn’t dress wounds, doesn’t fumigate [the cattle pen], doesn’t know fords, doesn’t know what it is [for the cattle] to have drunk, doesn’t know the road, is not skilled in pastures, milks dry, and shows no extra respect for the bulls who are fathers & leaders of the herd.
an11.17:1.4 | S: A cowherd with these eleven factors can’t maintain and expand a herd of cattle. | T: A cowherd endowed with these eleven factors is incapable of looking after a herd so that it prospers & grows.
an11.17:2.1 | S: In the same way, a mendicant with eleven qualities can’t achieve growth, improvement, or maturity in this teaching and training. | T: “A monk endowed with these eleven factors is incapable of attaining growth, increase, & abundance in this Dhamma-Vinaya.
an11.17:2.2 | S: What eleven? | T: Which eleven?
an11.17:2.3 | S: It’s when a mendicant doesn’t know form, is unskilled in characteristics, doesn’t pick out flies’ eggs, doesn’t dress wounds, doesn’t spread smoke, doesn’t know the ford, doesn’t know satisfaction, doesn’t know the trail, is not skilled in pastures, milks dry, and doesn’t show extra respect to senior mendicants of long standing, long gone forth, fathers and leaders of the Saṅgha. | T: There is the case where a monk is not well-versed in forms, unskilled in characteristics, doesn’t pick out flies’ eggs, doesn’t dress wounds, doesn’t fumigate, doesn’t know fords, doesn’t know what it is to have drunk, doesn’t know the road, is not skilled in pastures, milks dry, and shows no extra respect for the elder monks with seniority, who have been ordained long, who are fathers & leaders of the Community.
an11.17:3.1 | S: And how does a mendicant not know form? | T: “And how is a monk not well-versed in forms?
an11.17:3.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant doesn’t truly understand that all form is the four principal states, or form derived from the four principal states. | T: There is the case where a monk does not discern, as it actually is, that every form whatsoever is composed of the four great existents and the forms dependent on them.
an11.17:3.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant doesn’t know form. | T: This is how a monk is not well-versed in forms.
an11.17:4.1 | S: And how is a mendicant not skilled in characteristics? | T: “And how is a monk unskilled in characteristics?
an11.17:4.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant doesn’t understand that a fool is characterized by their deeds, and an astute person is characterized by their deeds. | T: There is the case where a monk does not discern, as it actually is, that a fool is characterized by his actions, a wise person is characterized by his actions.
an11.17:4.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant isn’t skilled in characteristics. | T: This is how a monk is unskilled in characteristics.
an11.17:5.1 | S: And how does a mendicant not pick out flies’ eggs? | T: “And how does a monk not pick out flies’ eggs?
an11.17:5.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant tolerates a sensual, malicious, or cruel thought that has arisen. They don’t give it up, dispel it, eliminate it, and obliterate it. | T: There is the case where a monk acquiesces with an arisen thought of sensuality. He does not abandon it, dispel it, demolish it, or wipe it out of existence. He acquiesces with an arisen thought of ill will… an arisen thought of harmfulness. He does not abandon it, dispel it, demolish it, or wipe it out of existence.
an11.17:5.3 | S: They tolerate any bad, unskillful qualities that have arisen. They don’t give them up, dispel them, eliminate them, and obliterate them. | T: He acquiesces with arisen evil, unskillful qualities. He doesn’t abandon them, dispel them demolish them, or wipe the out of existence.
an11.17:5.4 | S: That’s how a mendicant doesn’t pick out flies’ eggs. | T: This is how a monk doesn’t pick out flies’ eggs.
an11.17:6.1 | S: And how does a mendicant not dress wounds? | T: “And how does a monk not dress wounds?
an11.17:6.2 | S: When a mendicant sees a sight with their eyes, they get caught up in the features and details. | T: There is the case where a monk, on seeing a form with the eye, grasps at themes or details by which—
an11.17:6.3 | S: Since the faculty of sight is left unrestrained, bad unskillful qualities of covetousness and displeasure become overwhelming. They don’t practice restraint, they don’t protect the faculty of sight, and they don’t achieve its restraint. | T: as he dwells without restraint over the faculty of the eye—evil, unskillful qualities such as greed or distress might assail him. He doesn’t practice with restraint. He doesn’t practice for its restraint. He doesn’t protect the faculty of the eye.
an11.17:6.4 | S: When they hear a sound with their ears … | T: On hearing a sound with the ear…
an11.17:6.5 | S: When they smell an odor with their nose … | T: On smelling an odor with the nose…
an11.17:6.6 | S: When they taste a flavor with their tongue … | T: On tasting a flavor with the tongue…
an11.17:6.7 | S: When they feel a touch with their body … | T: On touching a tactile sensation with the body…
an11.17:6.8 | S: When they know an idea with their mind, they get caught up in the features and details. | T: On cognizing an idea with the intellect, he grasps at themes or details by which—
an11.17:6.9 | S: Since the faculty of the mind is left unrestrained, bad unskillful qualities of covetousness and displeasure become overwhelming. They don’t practice restraint, they don’t protect the faculty of the mind, and they don’t achieve its restraint. | T: as he dwells without restraint over the faculty of the intellect—evil, unskillful qualities such as greed or distress might assail him. He doesn’t practice for its restraint. He doesn’t protect the faculty of the intellect.
an11.17:6.10 | S: That’s how a mendicant doesn’t dress wounds. | T: This is how a monk doesn’t dress wounds.
an11.17:7.1 | S: And how does a mendicant not spread smoke? | T: “And how does a monk not fumigate?
an11.17:7.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant doesn’t teach others the Dhamma in detail as they learned and memorized it. | T: There is the case where a monk does not teach others in detail the Dhamma as he has heard and mastered it.
an11.17:7.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant doesn’t spread smoke. | T: This is how a monk doesn’t fumigate.
an11.17:8.1 | S: And how does a mendicant not know the ford? | T: “And how does a monk not know fords?
an11.17:8.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant doesn’t from time to time go up to those mendicants who are very learned—inheritors of the heritage, who have memorized the teachings, the monastic law, and the outlines—and ask them questions: | T: There is the case where a monk goes time & again to the monks who are learned, well-versed in the tradition, who have memorized the Dhamma, the Vinaya, and the Matikas, but doesn’t question them, doesn’t present them with his problems:
an11.17:8.3 | S: ‘Why, sir, does it say this? What does that mean?’ | T: ‘How is this, venerable sir? What is the meaning of this?’
an11.17:8.4 | S: Those venerables don’t reveal what is hidden, clarify what is unclear, and dispel doubt regarding the many doubtful matters. | T: These venerable ones do not reveal what has not been revealed to him, do not make plain what has not been made plain to him, do not resolve his doubts about the many teachings that might give rise to doubt.
an11.17:8.5 | S: That’s how a mendicant doesn’t know the ford. | T: This is how a monk doesn’t know fords.
an11.17:9.1 | S: And how does a mendicant not know satisfaction? | T: “And how does a monk not know what it is to have drunk?
an11.17:9.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant, when the teaching and training proclaimed by the Realized One are being taught, finds no inspiration in the meaning and the teaching, and finds no joy connected with the teaching. | T: There is the case where a monk, when the Dhamma-Vinaya proclaimed by the Tathagata is being taught, doesn’t gain knowledge of the meaning, doesn’t gain knowledge of the Dhamma, doesn’t gain joy connected with the Dhamma.
an11.17:9.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant doesn’t know satisfaction. | T: This is how a monk doesn’t know what it is to have drunk.
an11.17:10.1 | S: And how does a mendicant not know the trail? | T: “And how does a monk not know the road?
an11.17:10.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant doesn’t truly understand the noble eightfold path. | T: There is the case where a monk does not discern, as it actually is, the noble eightfold path.
an11.17:10.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant doesn’t know the trail. | T: This is how a monk doesn’t know the road.
an11.17:11.1 | S: And how is a mendicant not skilled in pastures? | T: “And how is a monk unskilled in pastures?
an11.17:11.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant doesn’t truly understand the four kinds of mindfulness meditation. | T: There is the case where a monk does not discern, as they actually are, the four frames of reference.
an11.17:11.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant is not skilled in pastures. | T: This is how a monk is unskilled in pastures.
an11.17:12.1 | S: And how does a mendicant milk dry? | T: “And how does a monk milk dry?
an11.17:12.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant is invited by a householder to accept robes, almsfood, lodgings, and medicines and supplies for the sick. But they don’t know moderation in accepting. | T: There is the case where a monk—when faithful householders invite him to accept gifts of cloth, alms food, lodgings, and medicinal requisites for curing the sick—knows no moderation in taking.
an11.17:12.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant milks dry. | T: This is how a monk milks dry.
an11.17:13.1 | S: And how does a mendicant not show extra respect to senior mendicants of long standing, long gone forth, fathers and leaders of the Saṅgha? | T: “And how does a monk show no extra respect for the elder monks with seniority, who have been ordained long, who are fathers & leaders of the Community?
an11.17:13.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant doesn’t consistently treat senior mendicants of long standing, long gone forth, fathers and leaders of the Saṅgha with kindness by way of body, speech, and mind, both in public and in private. | T: There is the case where a monk does not establish himself in bodily acts of goodwill, in public & in private, toward the elder monks with seniority, who have been ordained long, who are fathers & leaders of the Community. He does not establish himself in verbal acts of goodwill… in mental acts of goodwill, in public & in private, toward the elder monks with seniority, who have been ordained long, who are fathers & leaders of the Community.
an11.17:13.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant doesn’t show extra respect to senior mendicants of long standing, long gone forth, fathers and leaders of the Saṅgha. | T: (none)
an11.17:14.1 | S: A mendicant with these eleven qualities can’t achieve growth, improvement, or maturity in this teaching and training. | T: “A monk endowed with these eleven factors is incapable of attaining growth, increase, & abundance in this Dhamma-Vinaya.
an11.17:15.1 | S: A cowherd with eleven factors can maintain and expand a herd of cattle. | T: “Monks, a cowherd endowed with eleven factors is capable of looking after a herd so that it prospers & grows.
an11.17:15.2 | S: What eleven? | T: Which eleven?
an11.17:15.3 | S: It’s when a cowherd knows form, is skilled in characteristics, picks out flies’ eggs, dresses wounds, spreads smoke, knows the ford, knows satisfaction, knows the trail, is skilled in pastures, doesn’t milk dry, and shows extra respect to the bulls who are fathers and captains of the herd. | T: There is the case where a cowherd is well-versed in forms (appearances), skilled in characteristics, picks out flies’ eggs, dresses wounds, fumigates, knows fords, knows what it is to have drunk, knows the road, is skilled in pastures, doesn’t milk dry, and shows extra respect for the bulls who are fathers & leaders of the herd.
an11.17:15.4 | S: A cowherd with these eleven factors can maintain and expand a herd of cattle. | T: A cowherd endowed with these eleven factors is capable of looking after a herd so that it prospers & grows.
an11.17:16.1 | S: In the same way, a mendicant with eleven qualities can achieve growth, improvement, and maturity in this teaching and training. | T: “A monk endowed with these eleven factors is capable of attaining growth, increase, & abundance in this Dhamma-Vinaya.
an11.17:16.2 | S: What eleven? | T: Which eleven?
an11.17:16.3 | S: It’s when a mendicant knows form, is skilled in characteristics, picks out flies’ eggs, dresses wounds, spreads smoke, knows the ford, knows satisfaction, knows the trail, is skilled in pastures, doesn’t milk dry, and shows extra respect to senior mendicants of long standing, long gone forth, fathers and leaders of the Saṅgha. | T: There is the case where a monk is well-versed in forms, skilled in characteristics, picks out flies’ eggs, dresses wounds, fumigates, knows fords, knows what it is to have drunk, knows the road, is skilled in pastures, doesn’t milk dry, and shows extra respect for the elder monks with seniority, who have been ordained long, who are fathers & leaders of the Community.
an11.17:17.1 | S: And how does a mendicant know form? | T: “And how is a monk well-versed in forms?
an11.17:17.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant truly understands that all form is the four principal states, or form derived from the four principal states. | T: There is the case where a monk discerns, as it actually is, that every form whatsoever is composed of the four great existents and the forms dependent on them.
an11.17:17.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant knows form. | T: This is how a monk is well-versed in forms.
an11.17:18.1 | S: And how is a mendicant skilled in characteristics? | T: “And how is a monk skilled in characteristics?
an11.17:18.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant understands that a fool is characterized by their deeds, and an astute person is characterized by their deeds. | T: There is the case where a monk discerns, as it actually is, that a fool is characterized by his actions, a wise person is characterized by his actions.
an11.17:18.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant is skilled in characteristics. | T: This is how a monk is skilled in characteristics.
an11.17:19.1 | S: And how does a mendicant pick out flies’ eggs? | T: “And how does a monk pick out flies’ eggs?
an11.17:19.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant doesn’t tolerate a sensual, malicious, or cruel thought that has arisen, but gives it up, dispels it, eliminates it, and exterminates it. They don’t tolerate any bad, unskillful qualities that have arisen, but give them up, dispel them, eliminate them, and obliterate them. | T: There is the case where a monk does not acquiesce with an arisen thought of sensuality. He abandons it, dispels it, demolishes it, & wipes it out of existence. He does not acquiesce with an arisen thought of ill will… an arisen thought of harmfulness He abandons it, dispels it, demolishes it, & wipes it out of existence. He doesn’t acquiesce with arisen evil, unskillful qualities. He abandons them, dispels them, demolishes them, & wipes them out of existence.
an11.17:19.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant picks out flies’ eggs. | T: This is how a monk pick outs flies’ eggs.
an11.17:20.1 | S: And how does a mendicant dress wounds? | T: “And how does a monk dress wounds?
an11.17:20.2 | S: When a mendicant sees a sight with their eyes, they don’t get caught up in the features and details. | T: There is the case where a monk, on seeing a form with the eye, does not grasp at any theme or details by which—
an11.17:20.3 | S: If the faculty of sight were left unrestrained, bad unskillful qualities of covetousness and displeasure would become overwhelming. For this reason, they practice restraint, protecting the faculty of sight, and achieving its restraint. | T: if he were to dwell without restraint over the faculty of the eye—evil, unskillful qualities such as greed or distress might assail him. He practices with restraint. He practices for its restraint. He protects the faculty of the eye.
an11.17:20.4 | S: When they hear a sound with their ears … | T: On hearing a sound with the ear…
an11.17:20.5 | S: When they smell an odor with their nose … | T: On smelling an odor with the nose…
an11.17:20.6 | S: When they taste a flavor with their tongue … | T: On tasting a flavor with the tongue…
an11.17:20.7 | S: When they feel a touch with their body … | T: On touching a tactile sensation with the body…
an11.17:20.8 | S: When they know an idea with their mind, they don’t get caught up in the features and details. | T: On cognizing an idea with the intellect, he does not grasp at any theme or details by which—
an11.17:20.9 | S: If the faculty of mind were left unrestrained, bad unskillful qualities of covetousness and displeasure would become overwhelming. For this reason, they practice restraint, protecting the faculty of mind, and achieving its restraint. | T: if he were to dwell without restraint over the faculty of the intellect—evil, unskillful qualities such as greed or distress might assail him. He practices for its restraint. He protects the faculty of the intellect.
an11.17:20.10 | S: That’s how a mendicant dresses wounds. | T: This is how a monk dresses wounds.
an11.17:21.1 | S: And how does a mendicant spread smoke? | T: “And how does a monk fumigate?
an11.17:21.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant teaches others the Dhamma in detail as they learned and memorized it. | T: There is the case where a monk teaches others in detail the Dhamma as he has heard and mastered it.
an11.17:21.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant spreads smoke. | T: This is how a monk fumigates.
an11.17:22.1 | S: And how does a mendicant know the ford? | T: “And how does a monk know fords?
an11.17:22.2 | S: It’s when from time to time a mendicant goes up to those mendicants who are very learned—inheritors of the heritage, who have memorized the teachings, the monastic law, and the outlines—and asks them questions: | T: There is the case where a monk goes time & again to the monks who are learned, well-versed in the tradition, who have memorized the Dhamma, the Vinaya, and the Matikas. He questions them, presents them with his problems:
an11.17:22.3 | S: ‘Why, sir, does it say this? What does that mean?’ | T: ‘How is this, venerable sir? What is the meaning of this?’
an11.17:22.4 | S: Those venerables reveal what is hidden, clarify what is unclear, and dispel doubt regarding the many doubtful matters. | T: These venerable ones reveal what has not been revealed to him, make plain what has not been made plain to him, resolve his doubts about the many teachings that might give rise to doubt.
an11.17:22.5 | S: That’s how a mendicant knows the ford. | T: This is how a monk knows fords.
an11.17:23.1 | S: And how does a mendicant know satisfaction? | T: “And how does a monk know what it is to have drunk?
an11.17:23.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant, when the teaching and training proclaimed by the Realized One are being taught, finds inspiration in the meaning and the teaching, and finds joy connected with the teaching. | T: There is the case where a monk, when the Dhamma-Vinaya proclaimed by the Tathagata is being taught, gains knowledge of the meaning, gains knowledge of the Dhamma, gains joy connected with the Dhamma.
an11.17:23.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant knows satisfaction. | T: This is how a monk knows what it is to have drunk.
an11.17:24.1 | S: And how does a mendicant know the trail? | T: “And how does a monk know the road?
an11.17:24.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant truly understands the noble eightfold path. | T: There is the case where a monk discerns, as it actually is, the noble eightfold path.
an11.17:24.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant knows the trail. | T: This is how a monk knows the roads.
an11.17:25.1 | S: And how is a mendicant skilled in pastures? | T: “And how is a monk skilled in pastures?
an11.17:25.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant truly understands the four kinds of mindfulness meditation. | T: There is the case where a monk discerns, as they actually are, the four frames of reference.
an11.17:25.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant is skilled in pastures. | T: This is how a monk is skilled in pastures.
an11.17:26.1 | S: And how does a mendicant not milk dry? | T: “And how does a monk not milk dry?
an11.17:26.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant is invited by a householder to accept robes, almsfood, lodgings, and medicines and supplies for the sick. | T: There is the case where a monk—when faithful householders invite him to accept gifts of cloth, alms food, lodgings, and medicinal requisites for curing the sick—
an11.17:26.3 | S: And that mendicant knows moderation in accepting. | T: knows moderation in taking.
an11.17:26.4 | S: That’s how a mendicant doesn’t milk dry. | T: This is how a monk does not milk dry.
an11.17:27.1 | S: And how does a mendicant show extra respect to senior mendicants of long standing, long gone forth, fathers and leaders of the Saṅgha? | T: “And how does a monk show extra respect for the elder monks with seniority, who have been ordained long, who are fathers & leaders of the Community?
an11.17:27.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant consistently treats senior mendicants of long standing, long gone forth, fathers and leaders of the Saṅgha with kindness by way of body, speech, and mind, both in public and in private. | T: There is the case where a monk establishes himself in bodily acts of goodwill, in public & in private, toward the elder monks with seniority, who have been ordained long, who are fathers & leaders of the Community. He establishes himself in verbal acts of goodwill… in mental acts of goodwill, in public & in private, toward the elder monks with seniority, who have been ordained long, who are fathers & leaders of the Community.
an11.17:27.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant shows extra respect to senior mendicants of long standing, long gone forth, fathers and leaders of the Saṅgha. | T: (none)
an11.17:28.1 | S: A mendicant with these eleven qualities can achieve growth, improvement, or maturity in this teaching and training.” | T: “A monk endowed with these eleven factors is capable of attaining growth, increase, & abundance in this Dhamma-Vinaya.”
an11.17:28.2 | P: Sattamaṁ. | T: (none)


